| Then {DE: -1, DF: 1, FH: -1, AC: -1, AH: 1, BC: 1} + 1 x {DE: 1, DF: -1}
|    = {FH: -1, AC: -1, AH: 1, BC: 1}
We are left with a linear equation with coefficients {FH: -1, AC: -1, AH: 1, BC: 1} that gives ∠(AC,BC) = ∠(AH,FH)



019. | Angle Chasing to prove CG ∥ CH:
| Premise ∠(AC,AG) = ∠(CG,AC) gives a linear equation with coefficients {AC: -2, AG: 1, CG: 1}
| Premise ∠(CD,CH) = ∠(DH,CD) gives a linear equation with coefficients {CD: -2, CH: 1, DH: 1}
| Then {AC: -1, AG: 1/2, CG: 1/2} + -1/2 x {CD: -2, CH: 1, DH: 1}
|    = {AC: -1, AG: 1/2, CG: 1/2, CD: 1, CH: -1/2, DH: -1/2}
| Premise AD ∥ AC gives a linear equation with coefficients {AC: 1, AD: -1}
| Then {AC: -1, AG: 1/2, CG: 1/2, CD: 1, CH: -1/2, DH: -1/2} + 1 x {AC: 1, AD: -1}
|    = {AG: 1/2, CG: 1/2, CD: 1, CH: -1/2, DH: -1/2, AD: -1}
| Premise CD ∥ AD gives a linear equation with coefficients {AD: 1, CD: -1}
| Then {AG: 1/2, CG: 1/2, CD: 1, CH: -1/2, DH: -1/2, AD: -1} + 1 x {AD: 1, CD: -1}
|    = {AG: 1/2, CG: 1/2, CH: -1/2, DH: -1/2}
| Premise CG ∥ AG gives a linear equation with coefficients {AG: 1, CG: -1}
| Then {AG: 1/2, CG: 1/2, CH: -1/2, DH: -1/2} + -1/2 x {AG: 1, CG: -1}
|    = {CG: 1, CH: -1/2, DH: -1/2}
| Premise DH ∥ CH gives a linear equation with coefficients {CH: 1, DH: -1}
| Then {CG: 1, CH: -1/2, DH: -1/2} + -1/2 x {CH: 1, DH: -1}
|    = {CG: 1, CH: -1}
We are left with a linear equation with coefficients {CG: 1, CH: -1} that gives CG ∥ CH



021. | Ratio Chasing to prove AG:CG = CH:DH:
| Premise CG = AG gives a linear equation with coefficients {AG²: 1, CG²: -1}
| Premise DH = CH gives a linear equation with coefficients {CH²: 1, DH²: -1}
| Then {AG²: 1, CG²: -1} + -1 x {CH²: 1, DH²: -1}
|    = {AG²: 1, CG²: -1, CH²: -1, DH²: 1}
We are left with a linear equation with coefficients {AG²: 1, CG²: -1, CH²: -1, DH²: 1} that gives AG:CG = CH:DH



023. | Ratio Chasing to prove AC:CD = CG:CH:
| Premise DH = CH gives a linear equation with coefficients {CH²: 1, DH²: -1}
| Premise AC:CD = CG:DH gives a linear equation with coefficients {AC²: 1, CD²: -1, CG²: -1, DH²: 1}
| Then {CH²: 1, DH²: -1} + 1 x {AC²: 1, CD²: -1, CG²: -1, DH²: 1}
|    = {CH²: 1, AC²: 1, CD²: -1, CG²: -1}
We are left with a linear equation with coefficients {CH²: 1, AC²: 1, CD²: -1, CG²: -1} that gives AC:CD = CG:CH



026. | Angle Chasing to prove AG ∥ GH:
| Premise CG ∥ AG gives a linear equation with coefficients {AG: 1, CG: -1}
| Premise CG ∥ GH gives a linear equation with coefficients {CG: 1, GH: -1}
| Then {AG: 1, CG: -1} + 1 x {CG: 1, GH: -1}
|    = {AG: 1, GH: -1}
We are left with a linear equation with coefficients {AG: 1, GH: -1} that gives AG ∥ GH



029. | Ratio Chasing to prove AG:AH = BC:CE:
| Premise CG = AG gives a linear equation with coefficients {AG²: 1, CG²: -1}
| Premise AC:AH = BC:FH gives a linear equation with coefficients {AC²: 1, AH²: -1, BC²: -1, FH²: 1}
| Then {AG²: 1, CG²: -1} + 1 x {AC²: 1, AH²: -1, BC²: -1, FH²: 1}
|    = {AG²: 1, CG²: -1, AC²: 1, AH²: -1, BC²: -1, FH²: 1}
| Premise AC:CD = CG:DH gives a linear equation with coefficients {AC²: 1, CD²: -1, CG²: -1, DH²: 1}
| Then {AG²: 1, CG²: -1, AC²: 1, AH²: -1, BC²: -1, FH²: 1} + -1 x {AC²: 1, CD²: -1, CG²: -1, DH²: 1}
|    = {AG²: 1, AH²: -1, BC²: -1, FH²: 1, CD²: 1, DH²: -1}
| Premise CD:CE = DH:FH gives a linear equation with coefficients {CD²: 1, CE²: -1, DH²: -1, FH²: 1}
| Then {AG²: 1, AH²: -1, BC²: -1, FH²: 1, CD²: 1, DH²: -1} + -1 x {CD²: 1, CE²: -1, DH²: -1, FH²: 1}
|    = {AG²: 1, AH²: -1, BC²: -1, CE²: 1}
We are left with a linear equation with coefficients {AG²: 1, AH²: -1, BC²: -1, CE²: 1} that gives AG:AH = BC:CE



031. | Ratio Chasing to prove AD = BE:
| Premise BC = AC gives a linear equation with coefficients {AC²: 1, BC²: -1}
| Premise DH = CH gives a linear equation with coefficients {CH²: 1, DH²: -1}
| Then {AC²: 1, BC²: -1} + -1 x {CH²: 1, DH²: -1}
|    = {AC²: 1, BC²: -1, CH²: -1, DH²: 1}
| Premise AC:AH = BC:FH gives a linear equation with coefficients {AC²: 1, AH²: -1, BC²: -1, FH²: 1}
| Then {AC²: 1, BC²: -1, CH²: -1, DH²: 1} + -1 x {AC²: 1, AH²: -1, BC²: -1, FH²: 1}
|    = {CH²: -1, DH²: 1, AH²: 1, FH²: -1}
| Premise AD:CD = GH:CH gives a linear equation with coefficients {AD²: 1, CD²: -1, CH²: 1, GH²: -1}
| Then {CH²: -1, DH²: 1, AH²: 1, FH²: -1} + 1 x {AD²: 1, CD²: -1, CH²: 1, GH²: -1}
|    = {DH²: 1, AH²: 1, FH²: -1, AD²: 1, CD²: -1, GH²: -1}
| Premise AH:CE = GH:BE gives a linear equation with coefficients {AH²: 1, BE²: 1, CE²: -1, GH²: -1}
| Then {DH²: 1, AH²: 1, FH²: -1, AD²: 1, CD²: -1, GH²: -1} + -1 x {AH²: 1, BE²: 1, CE²: -1, GH²: -1}
|    = {DH²: 1, FH²: -1, AD²: 1, CD²: -1, BE²: -1, CE²: 1}
| Premise CD:CE = DH:FH gives a linear equation with coefficients {CD²: 1, CE²: -1, DH²: -1, FH²: 1}
| Then {DH²: 1, FH²: -1, AD²: 1, CD²: -1, BE²: -1, CE²: 1} + 1 x {CD²: 1, CE²: -1, DH²: -1, FH²: 1}
|    = {AD²: 1, BE²: -1}
We are left with a linear equation with coefficients {AD²: 1, BE²: -1} that gives AD = BE

End of proof.
